## ABSTRACT
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with redox-active metal sites and controllable crystalline structures make it possible to access the merits of highly-efficient electrode materials in electrochemical energy storage systems. However, most MOFs suffer from low capacitance and poor cycling stability that largely thwart their application. Herein, we present the holey graphene oxide (HGO) template strategy to prepare nano two-dimensional Ni(BDC) with HGO as both template and capping agent (denoted as Ni(BDC)- HGOx, $x=10$ , 20, 30, and 40 according to the added HGO amount). Structural analyses reveal that HGO can significantly inhibit the  Ni(BDC)  agglomeration,  thus  offering  a  high  ion-accessible  surface  area.  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  with  well-exposed  active  sites exhibits  a high  capacitance  of $1,115.6\ \mathsf{F}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathsf{A}^{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ in  6  M  KOH  aqueous,  1.8  times  that  of  bulk  Ni(BDC).  An  asymmetric supercapacitor with Ni(BDC)-HGO30 as a positive electrode and activated carbon as the opposing electrode delivers an energy density  of $52.5~\mathsf{W}\mathsf{\cdot h}\mathsf{\cdot k g}^{-1}$ and  a  power  density  up  to $18.0\ \mathsf{k W}\cdot\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ ,  with $92.5\%$ capacitance  retention  after  10,000  cycles. Galvanostatic intermittent titration technique and in situ electrochemical–Raman measurements were exploited to elucidate the electrochemical behavior of Ni(BDC)-HGO30. These results pave the way for the development of rationally tuned MOF materials for enhancing supercapacitor performances.
## KEYWORDS
holey graphene oxide, templated construction, nano MOFs, supercapacitor
## Introduction
Metal–organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  as  a  burgeoning  family  of crystalline porous materials assembled by inorganic and organic building  blocks  have  been  extensively  investigated  over  the  past decades  [1–3].  Benefiting  from  the  high  specific  surface  area, predictable pore size, well-dispersed active sites, adjustable redoxactive  sites,  and  controllable  chemical  environment,  MOFs  have drawn considerable attention as electrode materials in electrochemical energy storage systems (e.g., batteries, supercapacitors,  and  fuel  cells)  [4–10].  Particularly,  transition metal-based MOFs possessing superior electrolyte accessible areas, high-density,  and  unsaturated  surface  metal  sites  have  been identified  as  promising  candidates  for  supercapacitors  [11–14]. However,  the  relatively  low  reversibility  during  fast  chargedischarge  and  low  conductivity  in  most  MOFs  restricts  their efficiency as active electrode materials in capacitive energy storage [15].
To address these issues, exploitation of conductive MOFs [4, 5, 16] and tuning the MOF crystal orientation [17, 18], morphology [19, 20],  and  constituent  have  been  studied  [21].  But  these methods  experience  high-cost  of  ligands  synthesis  and  complex processes,  and  poor  electron  transfer  still  inevitably  exists.
Considering  that  nano  MOFs  provide  high-density  redox-active sites  and  promote  fast  electron  transfer,  the  design  of  welldispersed  nano  two-dimensional  (2D)  MOFs  provide  an opportunity for energy storage relative to their counterparts with those of bulk lamellar structures [22–25]. Nickel-based MOFs (NiMOFs) have been considered promising electrode materials due to their excellent pseudocapacitive performances [26, 27]. However, the aggregation and stacking of Ni-MOFs lead to low porosity and suppress the electron or ion transfer, which result in relatively low electrochemical performances. Furthermore, the redox reaction of the bulk Ni-MOFs cannot be fully completed in the rapid chargedischarge  process,  resulting  in  a  low-rate  capability  and  poor cycling stability.
Numerous  approaches  have  been  investigated  to  promote electrochemical energy storage performances and cycling stability of Ni-MOFs electrode materials, such as doping metal cations [28, 29],  mechanical  or  physicochemical  exfoliation  [30, 31],  and integrating with conductive nanostructures [32, 33]. Among them, Ni-MOFs integrated with conductive nanostructures have shown more prominence. In contrast to the graphene oxide (GO), holey GO (HGO)-based composites with large surface areas, abundant active  sites,  and  sufficient  cross-plane  channels  can  significantly facilitate the penetration of electrolyte ions into electrode materials and  rapid  charge  transfer  [34–36].  Based  on  the  following considerations,  we  hypothesized  that  the  HGO-templated construction  of $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ would  not  only  scale  down  the  bulk MOFs to the nanometer range but also accelerate the transmission of  electrons  and  electrolyte  ions,  and  thus  improving  the electrochemical performances and the cycling life: 1) HGO with sufficient  carboxyl  functional  groups  provides  large  accessible surface and more sites for nano $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ crystals formation; 2) HGO can significantly alleviate the limitation of poor conductivity of Ni-MOF and promote electron and electrolyte ions transport in the whole electrode; 3) HGO endows $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ better structural stability, leading to a longer cycle life; and 4) the hierarchical pore structures  in $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ and  HGO  provide  enough  space  for reducing the mass transfer resistance, thus improving the reaction kinetics.
Herein,  we  present  unique  advantages  of  the  HGO  template strategy to prepare Ni(BDC) with uniform particles (denoted as Ni(BDC)-HGOx, $x=10$ , 20, 30, and 40 according to the added HGO amount) electrode materials for supercapacitor (Fig. 1). The carboxyl  functional  groups  on  HGO  facilitate  the  formation  of nano $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ , and the partially reduced HGO can significantly improve  electron  and  ionic  conduction,  resulting  in  the improvement  of  supercapacitor  performance  and  cycling  life. Ni(BDC)-HGO30  shows  a  highly  efficient  capacitive  energy storage with a capacitance of $1,115.6~\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ at $1\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ , 1.8 times that of  bulk $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ .  For  further  practical  applications,  an asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) device using Ni(BDC)-HGO30 as the positive electrode and porous activated carbon (AC) as the opposing  electrode  delivers  a  maximum  energy  density  of  52.5 $\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at  the  power  density  of  791.1 $\mathbf{W}{\cdot}\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ,  with $92.5\%$ capacitance  retention  after  10,000  cycles.  More  importantly, galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) and in situ electrochemical-Raman  spectra  are  conducted  to  further  offer insight into the electrochemical behavior of Ni(BDC)-HGO30.
## 2    Experimental
## 2.1    Materials
The used materials here conclude ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(99\%$ , ALADDIN
Reagent  Co.,  Ltd.),  benzenedicarboxylic  acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}B D C},$ $98\%$ , ALADDIN  Reagent  Co.,  Ltd.),  ethanol  (Tianjin  Fuyu  Chemical Reagent $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ ., Ltd.), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, Tianjin Fuyu Chemical  Reagent  Co.,  Ltd.),  triethylamine  (TEA,  Macklin Reagent $\mathrm{Co}.$ ., Ltd.), KOH $(\geq85.0\%$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co.,  Ltd.),  graphite  (Qingdao  Everlasting  Benefit  Graphite  Co., Ltd.), ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ $(95\%-98\%$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent $\mathrm{Co}.$ , Ltd.), $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ (Sinopharm  Chemical  Reagent  Co.,  Ltd.),  HCl $36\%-38\%$ ,  Sinopharm  Chemical  Reagent  Co.,  Ltd.), ${\mathrm{NaNO}}_{3}$ $(\geq$ $99\%$ ,  Sinopharm  Chemical  Reagent  Co.,  Ltd.), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(30\%,$ Sinopharm  Chemical  Reagent $C0.$ ,  Ltd.),  acetylene  black (Mitsubishi Chemical Co., Ltd.), poly tetra fluoroethylene (PTFE, $60\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ,  Shanghai  Hesen  Electric $\mathrm{Co}.$ ,  Ltd.),  activated  carbon (Shanli  Chemical  Materials  Technology  Co.,  Ltd.),  and  liquid nitrogen  (Qingdao  Xinke  Chemical  Technology  Co.,  Ltd.). Deionized  (DI)  water  was  prepared  by  using  the  Heal  Force ultrapure  water  purification  system.  All  chemicals  and  solvents were used as received without further purification.
## 2.2    Instrumental
## 2.3    Synthesis of Ni(BDC)
The $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ was  prepared  via  a  facile  ultrasonic  method according  to  previous  reports  with  minor  modifications  [37]. Firstly, $0.75\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ was dissolved into the mixed solution of  DMF $32~\mathrm{mL},$ ),  ethanol $(2\ \mathrm{mL})$ ,  and  water $(2\ \mathrm{mL})$ under ultrasonication.  Subsequently,  0.75  mmol ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved  and $0.8~\mathrm{mL}$ TEA  was  added  to  the  mixed  solution  to obtain a colloidal suspension. Afterward, the colloidal solution was ultrasonicated for $6\mathrm{h}$ $\mathrm{\langle40\kHz\rangle}$ . Finally, Ni(BDC) was obtained via centrifugation, washed with ethanol 3 times, and dried in vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ .
## 2.4    Synthesis of HGO
GO  was  prepared  by  oxidation  of  natural  graphite  powder according to the modified Hummers’ method [38]. The synthesis of HGO was as follows: $50\upmu\mathrm{L}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ aqueous solution $(30\%)$ was added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ GO aqueous dispersion $(2\ \mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ in a 100 mL Teflon-lined autoclave. The mixture was sealed and heated at
$180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ and naturally cooled down to room temperature. Finally, HGO was immersed in distilled water and vacuum freezedried to get HGO. The procedure for obtaining RGO is the same as for HGO, except that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is not used.
## 2.5    Synthesis of Ni(BDC)-HGO
10,  20,  30,  and $40~\mathrm{mg}$ HGO  was  separately  dispersed  into  the mixed solution of DMF $(32~\mathrm{mL})$ ), ethanol $(2~\mathrm{mL})$ , and water (2 mL)  under  ultrasonication  for $^{\mathrm{~1~h~}}$ Subsequently, $0.75\mathrm{\mmol}$ ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in the solution, and 0.75 mmol $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ was added to the mixed solution under ultrasonication. After that, $0.8~\mathrm{mL}$ TEA was added to the mixed solution to obtain a colloidal suspension. After that, the colloidal solution was ultrasonicated for 6 h $(40\ \mathrm{kHz})$ . Finally, Ni(BDC) was obtained via centrifugation, washed with ethanol 3 times, and dried in vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 h.  The  as-prepared  samples  were  denoted  as  Ni(BDC)-HGOx, and $x$ stands for the added amount of HGO $(x=10,$ , 20, 30, and 40). Ni(BDC)-GO30 and Ni(BDC)-RGO30 were obtained as the same procedure only change HGO to GO or RGO.
## 2.6    ASC assemble method
The ACS device was fabricated by using Ni(BDC)-HGO30 coated onto  nickel  foam  as  a  positive  electrode  and  activated  carbon coated onto nickel foam as a negative electrode. Then, the positive electrode,  porous  separator  (impregnated  with  6  M  KOH electrolyte),  a  negative  electrode  and  a  battery  shrapnel  were stacked in sequence inside the battery shell. Finally, the assembled asymmetric  supercapacitor  was  obtained  by  pressing  under  10 Mpa for $30s$ .
## 2.7    Electrochemical characterization
The  measurements  of  cyclic  voltammetry  (CV),  galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy  (EIS)  in  a  typical  three-electrode  system  were conducted  on  a  CHI660E  workstation  containing  6  M  KOH aqueous  solution  as  electrolyte.  The  working  electrode  material was $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC}){\cdot}\mathrm{HGO}x$ . The counter and reference electrodes were Pt sheet and $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode, respectively. The work electrode ink  was  fabricated  via  uniformly  mixing  Ni(BDC)-HGOx, acetylene  black,  and  PTFE $(\mathrm{wt\%}$ ,  75:20:5)  in  a  small  amount  of ethanol. Then, the above ink was uniformly dripped coating onto Nickel foam ( $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ in area) to form a thin work electrode under a pressure of 8–10 Mpa for $30~\mathsf{s}.$ . The mass loading was $\sim2~\mathrm{mg}$ on the working electrode for further tests. The specific capacitance $\scriptstyle\left.\mathrm{\phantom{.}}C,\right.$ $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ) was calculated according to the following Eq. (1) based on GCD tests:
$$
C={\frac{I\Delta t}{m\Delta V}}
$$
where $I$ represents the discharge current (A), $\Delta\varDelta t$ represents the discharge  time  (s), $m$ represents  the  mass  of  electroactive components $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{g})$ , and $\Delta V$ represents the potential window $(V)$ .
A  two-electrode  cell  configuration  was  used  to  measure  the performance  of  an  asymmetric  supercapacitor  in  6  M  KOH aqueous  electrolyte  solution  on  a  CHI660E  workstation.  In  the assembled ASC device, the mass matching of positive and negative electrode materials is balanced via the following Eq. (2):
$$
\frac{m^{+}}{m^{-}}=\frac{C^{-}\Delta V^{-}}{C^{+}\Delta V^{+}}
$$
herein, $m,\ C,$ and $\Delta V$ represent  the  mass  of  electroactive components,  specific  capacitance,  and  discharge  potential window,  respectively.  The  superscript  of $^+$ and  − represent  the positive and negative electrodes, respectively.
The mass ratio between the negative and positive electrodes was set to 4:1 $(8~\mathrm{mg}{:}2~\mathrm{mg})$ . The power density and energy density are calculated according to the following Eqs. (3) and (4):
$$
\begin{array}{c}{E=\cfrac{C\Delta V^{2}}{2\times3.6}}\\ {P=\cfrac{3600E}{\Delta t}}\end{array}
$$
where $C$ is the specific capacitance of the device obtained $(\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}),E$ is the energy density $\left(\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\right)$ , $P$ is the power density $(\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})_{\cdot}$ , $\Delta t$ is the discharge time of the device (s), and $\Delta V$ is the working voltage window (V).
The  imaginary  part $C^{\prime}\left(\omega\right)$ in  electrochemical  impedance spectroscopy was calculated as following Eq. (5):
$$
C^{\prime}\left(\omega\right)=\frac{Z^{\prime}\left(\omega\right)}{\left(\omega\right)\left\vert Z\left(\omega\right)\right\vert^{2}}
$$
where $Z^{\prime}(\omega)$ is  the  real  part  of  the  electrochemical  impedance $(Z(\omega))$ ,  and $\omega$ as  angular  frequency  is  given  by  a  function  of $\omega=2\pi f\ (f$ is  the  frequency).  The  characteristic  relaxation  time constants $(\tau_{0})$ are the reciprocal of the frequency corresponding to the highest point of the normalized $C^{\prime\prime}\left(\omega\right)$ curves.
The  voltammetric  response  of  an  electrode  at  various  sweep rates can be generally expressed as following Eq. (6):
$$
i=a\nu^{b}
$$
where $i$ is the peak current (A), $\nu$ is the scan rate $\mathrm{(mV}{\cdot}s^{-1})$ , $a$ and $b$ are arbitrary coefficients. In general, when the $b$ value approaches 1, the capacitive process dominates. While the $b$ value is about 0.5, the  system  is  mainly  controlled  by  the  diffusion  process.  This process  was  done  by  taking  the $\log$ of  both  sides  of  Eq.  (6)  to obtain Eq. (7) for the slope as fitted parameter $b$ :
$$
\log i=\log a+b\log\nu
$$
where $a$ and $b$ are fitted parameters. The data were available when the  fitting $R^{2}>0.97.$ .  Furthermore,  the  contribution  of pseudocapacitance  at  a  given  scanning  rate  was  quantified  as following Eq. (8):
$$
i(V)/\nu^{1/2}=k_{1}\nu^{1/2}+k_{2}
$$
where $k_{\mathrm{1}}$ and $k_{2}$ are adjustable parameters obtained by linear fitting of $i(V)/\nu^{1/2}$ and $\nu^{1/2}$ in Eq. (8). $k_{1}\nu$ at each particular voltage is the capacitive  contribution  to  the  current.  Thus,  by  calculating  the slope $k_{\mathrm{1}}$ for various potentials and scan rates, the CV integrated area is subsequently fitted according to Eq. (8), which represents the capacitive contribution.
The GITT measurements were carried out by applying a pulsed constant current on a full cell with the current density of $0.1\mathrm{\A{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ for $^{30\mathrm{~s,~}}$ followed  by $30\mathrm{~s~}$ relaxation  to  reach  an  equilibrium voltage  on  a  Land-CT2001A  tester  (Wuhan,  China).  The  OH diffusion  coefficients $\left(D,\ c m\cdot s^{-1}\right)$ were  calculated  based  on  the following Eq. (9) [39, 40]:
$$
D={\frac{4}{\pi\tau}}\left({\frac{m_{\mathrm{B}}V_{\mathrm{M}}}{M_{\mathrm{B}}S}}\right)^{2}\left({\frac{\Delta E_{\mathrm{s}}}{\Delta E_{\mathrm{t}}}}\right)^{2}
$$
where $\tau$ is the relaxation time (s), $m_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the quality of the electrode material  (g); $V_{\mathrm{{M}}}$ is  the  Molar  volume  of  electrode  material $(\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ); $M_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Molar mass of electrode material $(\mathbf{g}),s$ is the electrode  contact  area $(\mathrm{cm}^{2}),\ \Delta E_{\mathrm{s}}$ is  the  steady  state  potential change (V) by the current pulse, and $\Delta E_{\mathrm{{t}}}$ is the potential change (V) during the constant current pulse after the $I R$ drops.
## 3    Results and discussion
## 3.1    Characterizations
