}.$ the  binding  energies  of  Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ in $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC}){\cdot}\mathrm{HGO}x$ exhibited  a noticeable upshift of about $0.8~\mathrm{eV}$ , revealing the decrement of Ni chemical  potentials  in $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC}){\cdot}\mathrm{HGO}x$ (Fig. 2(e)).  Such  a decrement  can  be  ascribed  to  the  strong  interactions  between Ni(BDC) and HGO, which is beneficial for electron transfer [45]. Furthermore,  the  high-resolution  XPS  spectra  for  O  1s  of Ni(BDC) show two peaks centered at 531.4 and $533.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which are  attributed  to  the  C–O  and  Ni–O  groups,  respectively. However,  there  is  a  shift $(0.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ of  O  1s  in $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC}){\cdot}\mathrm{HGO}x$ compared to that in $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}$ due to the interaction of Ni(BDC) with HGO (Fig. 2(f)).
The  sufficient  carboxyl  groups  on  HGO  can  act  as  capping agents  that  facilitate  to  control  the  particle  sizes  of $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}$ to nanostructure  [46–48].  As  shown  in  field-emission  scanning electron  microscopy  (FE-SEM)  images,  Ni(BDC)  shows  bulk moephology with a particle size of $\sim4\upmu\mathrm{m}.$ , HGO presents flexible, gauze-like  and  wrinkled  sheets,  and  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  exhibits well-dispersed  Ni(BDC)  on  HGO  (Figs.  3(a)–3(c)).  Ni(BDC)- HGOx with HGO amount less than $30~\mathrm{mg}$ exhibit small particles on  the  surface  of  HGO,  while  large  particles  are  formed  in Ni(BDC)-HGO40, which also verify the change of specific surface area (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images show that bulk $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ stacks with large particle sizes  (Fig. 3(d))  and  HGO  possesses  the  thickness  of  around  7 layers  and  wrinkles  on  the  surface  (Fig. 3(e) and Fig. S9  in  the ESM),  while  in  Ni(BDC)-HGO30,  HGO  template-constructed nano $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ are uniformly grown on HGO with a particle size of $\sim60\mathrm{nm}$ and a thickness of $4\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 3(f), Figs. S10 and S11 in the ESM). Obviously, due to the HGO templated construction of nano $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ improving  the  BET  specific  surface  area  of Ni(BDC)-HGO30,  more  surface  redox  sites  of  Ni(BDC)  are exposed.  These  results  illustrate  that  the $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC}){\cdot}\mathrm{HGO}x$ materials are successfully constructed, and offer great potential as electrode material for electrochemical devices.
## 3.2    Electrochemical performances
We  initially  evaluated  the  electrochemical  performance  of  asprepared samples in 6 M KOH via a three-electrode measurement system. As can be seen in Fig. 4(a), the CV curves of $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}.$ - HGOx on NF substrate show distinct nonrectangular shapes with redox  peak  pairs  over  the  potential  window  of $_{0-0.6\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Hg/HgO)}$ at a scan rate of $10\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . The NF substrate with low capacitance mainly serves as conductive electronic support to fully enable  the  hybrid  material's  electrochemical  activity  (Fig. S12  in the ESM). Faradaic redox peaks can be assigned to the change in the  oxidation  states  of  the $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/N i^{3+}}$ during  the  electrochemical reactions. The reduction and oxidation peaks in CV curves of the Ni(BDC)-HGOx electrodes  slightly  move  at  various  scan  rates from 5 to $50\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (Fig. S13 in the ESM), and the shape of CV curves  is  maintained  even  at  a  high  sweep  rate,  demonstrating superior reversibility of Ni(BDC)-HGOx. In addition, GCD curves show the non-linear triangular charge-discharge plots, indicating the typical battery-type activity of Ni(BDC)-HGOx (Fig. S14 in the ESM). The nearly symmetrical charge and discharge behaviors of Ni(BDC)-HGOx at a current density of $1\ \mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ over a potential window of $0{-}0.45\mathrm{V}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Hg/HgO)}$ indicates that $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC}){\cdot}\mathrm{HGO}x$ electrode  materials  are  highly  reversible  during  charging  and discharging process (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The EIS of $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}$ $\mathrm{\Pi~{~HGOx~}~}$ electrodes in the frequency range from 0.01 and $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ were measured at the open circuit potential. The EIS of $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}$ - $\mathrm{HGO}x$ electrodes are fitted well to the modified Randle’s circuit, and the Nyquist plots of Ni(BDC)-HGO30 show smaller intercept on  the  real  axis  and  semi-circle  diameter  than  other  electrodes, demonstrating that Ni(BDC)-HGO30 with lower resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ and internal resistance $(R_{s})$ is beneficial to electron transfer (Fig. 4(b), Fig. S16 and Table S5 in the ESM). In addition, all Ni(BDC)- $\mathrm{\Pi~{~HGOx~}~}$ electrodes  show  lower $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ and $R_{s}$ than $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ ,  which further  illustrates  that  HGO  can  significantly  enhance  the electrode  conductivity,  charge  transfer,  and  ion  diffusion. Ni(BDC)-HGO30 electrode presents the largest slope in the lowfrequency  region  associated  with  the  Warburg  impedance $(R_{\mathrm{w}})$ among the above-synthesized samples, indicating it possesses the lowest  ion  diffusion  resistance  and  the  fastest  ion  diffusion. Moreover, the relaxation time $(\tau_{0})$ represents the minimum time required  to  discharge  all  the  energy  from  the  device  with  an efficiency  of  greater  than $50\%$ .  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  exhibits  the relaxation  time  constant  of $0.15\ s$ which  is  lower  than  that  of Ni(BDC)  (4.64  s),  Ni(BDC)-HGO10  (0.83  s),  Ni(BDC)-HGO20 $\left(0.38\ s\right)$ ,  and  Ni(BDC)-HGO40  (0.32  s),  indicating  Ni(BDC)- HGO30  possesses  the  high-speed  ion  diffusion  and  transport behavior  and  thus  profiting  to  promote  the  electrochemical kinetics  and  rate  performance  (Fig. 4(c)).  Ni(BDC)-HGOx electrodes  show  the  specific  capacitance  at  different  current densities  with  first  rising  and  then  falling  with  the  increasing amount of HGO (Fig. S17 in the ESM). Due to the well- dispersed active sites and the high specific area, Ni(BDC)-HGO30 has the highest  specific  capacitance  calculated  by  the  longest  discharge time  among  all  the  samples,  which  is  consistent  with  the  CV results.  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  exhibits  a  remarkably  high  specific capacitance  of $1,115.6\ \mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ at $1\ {\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ ,  which  is  far  higher  than $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ $(610.2~\mathrm{Fcdotg^{-1}})$ , and the specific capacitance can be up to $618.7\mathrm{Fcdotg^{-1}}$ at $20\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
To further elucidate the advantage of HGO, RGO was obtained under  hydrothermal  condition  at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .  RGO exhibits fewer defects than HGO as a decreased ratio of $I_{\mathrm{D}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{G}}$ (HGO vs. RGO, 1.868 vs. 1.746) (Fig. S18 in the ESM). The highresolution  XPS  spectra  for  O  1s  also  reveal  that  RGO  with  few carboxyl groups, which is unfavorable for the formation of nano Ni(BDC) (Fig. S19 in the ESM). We also utilized RGO as template to  synthesize  Ni(BDC)-RGO30.  XPS  spectra  reveal  that  the binding  energies  of  Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ in  Ni(BDC)-RGO30  exhibited  an upshift of $\sim0.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ compared to Ni(BDC), revealing the strong interactions  between $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ and  RGO  (Fig. S20  in  the  ESM). However,  FE-SEM  image  of  Ni(BDC)-RGO30  shows  a  bulk particle covered with wrinkled RGO on the surface of Ni(BDC) (Fig. S21 in the ESM), and Ni(BDC)-RGO30 exhibits lower BET surface area than Ni(BDC)-HGO30 (Fig. S22 in the ESM). Then, the  electrochemical  performances  of  Ni(BDC)-RGO30  were analyzed  from  GCD  and  CV  curves  (Figs.  S23  and  S24  in  the ESM). The specific capacitance of Ni(BDC)-RGO30 shows a fast decrease with the current density prolonged (Fig. 4(d)), which can be ascribed to RGO promoting the conductivity but alleviating the ion diffusion (Fig. S25 in the ESM). Ni(BDC)-HGO30 shows high Coulombic efficiency and excellently stable cycling performance at a  current  density  of $5\ \mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (with $83\%$ retention  of  its  initial capacitance  after  5,000  cycles  of  charging  and  discharging)  that outperforms $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ (with $43\%$ retention  of  its  initial capacitance  after  1,500  cycles)  (Fig. 4(e))  and  the  structural morphology  of  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  is  almost  unchanged  (Fig. S26 in the ESM), indicating the HGO can significantly increase longterm  electrochemical  stability  of  Ni(BDC).  The  specific capacitance and stability of Ni(BDC)-HGO30 electrode outperforms  those  reported  on  Ni-MOF-based  SC  electrodes, which is listed in Table S6 in the ESM.
We  assembled  ASC  cells  with  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  positive electrodes  and  AC  negative  electrodes  in  a  6  M  KOH  aqueous electrolyte  (denoted  to  Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC).  As  displayed  in Fig. 5(a),  the  CV  curves  of  the  AC  and  Ni(BDC)-HGO30 electrode  show  that  the  working  voltage  window  of  assembled
ASC  device  can  be  up  to $1.6\mathrm{~V~}$ .  The  shape  of  Ni(BDC)- HGO30//AC  ASC  CV  curves  are  retained  at  various  potential windows ranging from 1.3 to $1.6\mathrm{V}$ in $6.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH electrolyte at a scan  rate  of $10\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\ s^{-1};$ ,  implying  that  this  ASC  possesses  good reversibility up to $1.6\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. S27 in the ESM). The CV curves of Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC ASC cell are almost unchanged at various scan rates, indicating that the Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC cell has an excellent  rate  performance  (Fig. S28  in  the  ESM).  All  the  GCD curves perform almost symmetrical at different current densities, revealing  that  the  Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC  cell  has  excellent reversibility  of  energy  storage  and  possesses  a  high  Coulombic efficiency (Fig. S29 in the ESM). As shown in the EIS results, the impedance  spectra  of  Ni(BDC)//AC  and  Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC are  fitted  well  to  the  modified  Randle’s  circuit  (Fig. S30  in  the ESM). Nyquist plots reveal that Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC possesses smaller  semi-circle  diameter  and  intercept  on  the  real  axis  than Ni(BDC)//AC,  demonstrating  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  with  lower $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ (Ni(BDC)-HGO30 vs. Ni(BDC)//AC, 0.66 $\Omega$ vs. $\phantom{-}0.87~\Omega$ ) and $R_{s}$ (Ni(BDC)-HGO30  vs.  Ni(BDC)//AC,  0.46  Ω  vs. $1.09\ \Omega$ )  than Ni(BDC)//AC. These results indicate that Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC ASC  has  low  charge  transfer  resistance  and  high  diffusion coefficient,  which  can  promote  the  kineticsof  electrochemical reactions and improve supercapacitor performance.
The  Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC  show  the  specific  capacitance  of $150.9\mathrm{Fcdotg^{-1}}$ at $1\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and $94.1\ \mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ at $10\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which is superior to $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}$ $\cdot109.4\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , and $30.7\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) (Fig. S31 in  the  ESM).  The  durability  of  the  as-prepared  Ni(BDC)- HGO30//AC ASC cell reveals that the capacitance still kept $92.5\%$ of  the  initial  value  after  10,000  cycles  at ${5}\ \mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and  Coulombic efficiencies  of  \~ $99\%$ (Fig. 5(b)),  demonstrating  the  highly electrochemical  reversibility  of  Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC,  which outperforms  most  MOF-  or  MOF  hybrid-based  ASCs  (Fig. 5(c) and  Table  S7  in  the  ESM).  The  Ragone  plot  obtained  via discharging  curves  of  the  Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC  ASC  has highlighted  its  energy  density  vs.  power  density  (Fig. 5(d)).  The Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC ASC delivers a high energy density of 52.5 $\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at  a  power  density  of $791.1~\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and  still  keep  10.9 $\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at  a  higher  power  density  of $18.0\ \mathrm{\kW{\cdotkg}^{-1}}$ ,  which outperforms  most  of  Ni-MOF-based  ASCs  reported  in  the literature,  such  as  CoZnNi-MOF//PEDOT-AC  [49],  GMLEG@Ni-MOF//AC [50], ${\mathrm{NiCo}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}4@\$ Ni-MOF//AC [51], NiO/Ni
MOF-25//AC  [52],  CoNi-MOF//AC  [17],  Ni-MOF//AC  [53], NiCo-MOF//AC  [54],  NiCo-MOF/NF//AC  [55],  Ni-MOF/rGO300//AC [56], 2D Ni-MOF/C-CNTs//AC [23], $\mathrm{\mathsf{a}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $\mathrm{NDs/RGO//Co_{3}O_{4}}$ NDs/RGO  [57], $\mathrm{GdMnO_{2}/N i(O H)_{2}//P V A/}$ $\mathrm{KOH}//\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{GO}$ [58],  ML-80//MG-5  [59],  and $\mathrm{PANI/Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{\it x}//}$ CPC [60] et al. (the specific data are listed in Table S8 in the ESM). The high specific capacitance and robust electrochemical stability make  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  promising  as  an  advanced  electrode candidate for supercapacitors.
## 3.3    Mechanism
To  better  understand  the  energy  storage  mechanism,  we calculated  the  relationship  between  the  square  root  of  scan  rate and oxidative and reductive peak currents based on the CV curve of Ni(BDC)-HGO30 in the three-electrode system (Fig. S32 in the ESM). The $b$ values of Ni(BDC) and Ni(BDC)-HGO30 based on the  CV  curves  are  slightly  higher  than  0.5,  suggesting  that  their electrochemical  behaviors  mainly  follow  a  diffusion-controlled charge storage mechanism. The capacitive-controlled contributions of Ni(BDC)-HGO30 exhibit gradual upward trends from $64.6\%$ to $97.1\ \%$ when the scan rate increased from 5 to 100 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}{\cdot}s^{-1};$ , which is high higher than that of Ni(BDC) (Fig. S33 in the ESM).  The  high  capacitive-controlled  contribution  of $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}.$ - HGO30 can be ascribed to the sufficient number of active sites, conducting  graphene  network,  and  short  diffusion  length  of Ni(BDC)-HGO30, which are beneficial for fast redox kinetics for the charge-discharge process.
GITT and in situ Raman spectra were conducted to investigate the diffusion-controlled and capacitive-controlled process, respectively. To further study the advantage of Ni(BDC)-HGO30, we also leveraged GO as a template to construct Ni(BDC)-GO30. GO  exhibits  the  same  effect  as  HGO  to  promote  the  100  facet growth  of  Ni(BDC)  (Fig. S34  in  the  ESM),  but  Ni(BDC)-GO30 has a relatively lower specific surface area and larger particle size of Ni(BDC) than Ni(BDC)-HGO30 (Figs. S35 and S36 in the ESM). The kinetics of OH− solid-state diffusion of Ni(BDC), Ni(BDC)- GO30,  and  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  are  determined  by  GITT measurements  (Fig. 6(a) and Fig. S37  in  the  ESM).  The  OH− insertion leads to a phase transition in Ni(BDC), Ni(BDC)-GO30, and Ni(BDC)-HGO30 as verified with a sudden slope change of the GITT curves (Fig. $\left.6(\mathrm{b})\right|$ ) [61], and the diffusion coefficients of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ were calculated from the marked area with less volatility. The average  diffusion  coefficient  of  Ni(BDC)-HGO30  during  the charging process is $6.4\times10^{-8}\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1};$ , which outperforms Ni(BDC) $(3.8~\times~10^{-9}~\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}s^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC}){\cdot}\mathrm{GO}30$ $(1.8\times10^{-9}\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}s^{-1}).$ revealing that OH− diffusion in Ni(BDC)-HGO30 is much faster than  that  in  other  samples  (Fig. $6(\mathrm{c})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ),  which  is  also  observed during  the  discharging  process  (Fig. S38  in  the  ESM). Interestingly,  Ni(BDC)-GO30  shows  slower  OH− diffusion  than $\mathrm{{Ni(BDC)}}.$ , indicating that GO with fewer defects and a low specific area is unfeasible for efficient ion transport (Fig. 1(a) and Fig. S35 in the ESM). These results illustrate that HGO template Ni(BDC) can  significantly  accelerate  the  electrolyte  ion  transport  by  its highly continuous network of open channels [62]. Moreover, to further obtain insight into the energy storage mechanism during the  capacitive-controlled  behavior  of  Ni(BDC)-GO30, in  situ electrochemical-Raman  spectra  were  measured  via  homemade equipment (Fig. 6(d) and Fig. S39 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 6(e), during the whole CV tests in the Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC ASC cell (the potential increases from 0 to $1.6\mathrm{V}$ and then fall to $0\mathrm{V}.$ ), two  new  Raman  peaks  centered  at  555  and $475~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ with  the potential  increasing  from  0  to $1.6\mathrm{~V~}$ appears,  which  can  be ascribed to the vibrations of Ni–O bonds in NiOOH [63, 64]. The in situ Raman spectra recover to the initial state with the potential decreasing  from  1.6  to $0\mathrm{~V~}_{:}$ ,  indicating  the  Ni(BDC)-HGO30 electrode  is  fully  electro-chemical  reversible.  These  results demonstrate  the  change  in  the  oxidation  states  of  the $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/N i^{3+}}$ (Ni(BDC) to NiOOH) during the capacitive-controlled electrochemical  energy  storage  leading  to  a  highly  efficient supercapacitor.  Therefore, ${\mathrm{Ni}}({\mathrm{BDC}})–{\mathrm{HGO}}30$ as  a  superior electrode  material  for  supercapacitor  mainly  experience  two processes:  the $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ diffuses  into  nano $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ (diffusioncontrolled  process)  and  reacts  with $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to  NiOOH  (capacitivecontrolled process) (Fig. 6(f)). These processes can be represented by the following Eqs. (10) and (11) [65, 66]:
$$
\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)(O H)_{2}+O H^{-}=N i(I I)(O H)_{2}(O H)_{a d}+e^{-}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})(\mathrm{OH})_{2}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{III})(\mathrm{OOH})+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{e}^{-}
$$
## 4    Conclusions
In conclusion, we report the holey graphene oxide template nano Ni(BDC)  strategy  for  achieving  highly  efficient  electrochemical energy storage. It is demonstrated that Ni(BDC)-HGO30 exhibits effectively higher electrochemical performance than bulk Ni(BDC) due to the advantage of holey graphene oxide and nano Ni(BDC). For  further  application,  a  Ni(BDC)-HGO30//AC  asymmetric supercapacitor  was  assembled,  which  delivers  a  high  energy density of $52.5\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1};$ , a high power density of $18.0\mathrm{kW\cdotkg^{-1}}$ , and a  capacitance  retention  of $92.5\%$ after  10,000  cycles  at $5\ \mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore,  GITT  measurements  were  conducted  to  elucidate that  the  porous  HGO  improves  the  OH− diffusion  behavior Ni(BDC)-HGO30  during  charging  and  discharging,  and in  situ electrochemical-Raman  spectra  reveal  insight  into  the  capacitive behavior of Ni(BDC)-HGO30. Such HGO template MOF down to the nanoscale level strategy and in situ analytical technique will help  in  better  understanding  the  ion  diffusion  and  electron transfer in pseudocapacitive materials and makes MOFs become promising candidates for other electrochemical materials.
